   <h2 class="margin-bottom">Project Process & Guidance</h2>
<h4>Portfolio Management (PREP)</h4>
<p>PREP stands for Project & Resource Engagement Process​​ and service as a centralized project request and prioritization process supported by a governance framework to align people and financial resources to projects supporting our strategic objectives.​​</p>
<p class="margin-bottom"><a class="external">Learn more about PREP</a></p>
<h4>Project Initiation</h4>
<p>All projects that cross departments for resources or assistance should use the formal process of project governance and oversight to maintain continuity across processes and more effectively manage the project from a risk and business complexity associated with projects. The Project and Resource Engagement Process (PREP) is the name of the project request process and is managed within TeamDynamix.</p>
<p>A Project Request should be initiated by submitting a request via the Create New Project Request service in TeamDynamix.​​</p>
<p class="margin-bottom"><a class="external">Learn more about initiation</a></p>
<h4>Project Planning</h4>
<p>During project planning, the project scope is defined and described with greater specificity as more information about the project is known.  Existing risks, assumptions, and constraints are analyzed for completeness and added or updated as necessary.​</p>
<p class="margin-bottom"><a class="external">Learn more about planning</a></p>
<h4>Project Execution</h4>
<p><strong>Project Management Office (PMO)</strong></p>
<p>The PMO uses a SharePoint list called Issue Tracker to track project issues.</p>
<p>Issue tracking is necessary to identify and track to resolve project issues. Issues can be as simple as questions and as complex as a testing issue requiring coding and/or process fix.​</p>
<p class="margin-bottom"><a class="external">Learn more about project execution</a></p>
<h4>Project Deployment</h4>
<p>Deployment typically occurs after User Acceptance Testing is complete. During this stage, cutover activities such as stakeholder training/communication, CAB submission/approval, environment preparation for go-live, and go-live occur.  On some projects, multiple deployment phases/waves may occur to minimize disruption to stakeholders and reduce the impact to project support staff and IMS Service Desk.​</p>
<p class="margin-bottom"><a class="external">Learn more about project deployment</a></p>
<h4>Project Closeout</h4>
<p>Project Closeout marks the completion of all project phases and ensures a smooth transition to ongoing operations. Activities include finalizing financials, verifying outcomes, transferring knowledge to sustainment teams, and documenting lessons learned. The process also confirms schedule completion, communicates results to stakeholders, and concludes with formal approvals and team recognition for a successful delivery.​</p>
<p class="margin-bottom"><a class="external">Learn more about project closeout</a></p>
